9.12 Limitations of the Proposed Rule
The rule is designed as an interpretable first-line control rather than a complete cybersecurity solution.
Several limitations remain:

an attacker or unsafe agent may embed suspicious content directly rather than using an external file;
external files may sometimes be legitimate;
a human-review process requires operational ownership and response-time standards;
file contents should be preserved for audit rather than deleted immediately after a blocked release;
additional controls may be needed upstream to reduce repeated propagation.

The proposed control should therefore be treated as a targeted intervention at the most reliable release gateway, not as a replacement for broader system monitoring.






WarningRecommended Intervention



The recommended intervention is a strengthened SaidIT pre-post validation gate.
Any agent-generated public post that uses an external content-source file should be blocked from direct publication and routed to human review.
This rule is supported by the historical evidence:

108 SaidIT posts are observed;
105 posts store embedded text directly;
only 3 posts reference external content-source files;
those 3 posts correspond exactly to HiddenOrca, MellowOtter, and SwiftWren;
all 3 suspicious posts passed through the existing pre-post check without being blocked.

The proposed rule would have intercepted the three historical suspicious releases while leaving the standard workflow unchanged for most ordinary posts.

11.2 Recommended Future Improvements
The following improvements should be considered in future system design:

preserve blocked files and related logs for forensic review;
add content scanning before public release;
record the reason for every validation decision;
alert system administrators when repeated instruction-file handoffs exceed a threshold;
introduce stricter controls for self-loop delegation;
monitor whether the same agent appears repeatedly across suspicious file families;
require explicit reviewer approval before publishing any external-file content.

4.1 Data Preparation
The raw JSON will be parsed with jsonlite and flattened from its nested rounds → agent outputs → communications structure into a set of tidy analysis tables:

a communications table, with one row per message (timestamp, sender, channel, and text);
an internal-state table, with one row per non-null deliberation event;
a declared-actions table, with one row per agent per round;
an environment table of hourly stock prices, market sentiment, and media events.

Timestamps will be parsed with lubridate to derive date, hour-of-day, and a crisis-day flag. Several analytical variables will then be engineered:

classification of every message into internal, official-public, or unofficial-public channel classes;
embargo-relevance flags for messages naming HarborCrest, CivicLoom, or the merger;
directed edge lists (agent → channel and agent → agent), computed separately for the normal-operations baseline and the crisis day;
behavioural baselines, using each agent’s pre-crisis mean and standard deviation of hourly message volume to standardise activity into z-scores;
an oversight indicator recording, for every round, whether any Judge message was observed.

All derived tables will be precomputed and saved as .rds files so the Shiny application loads instantly rather than re-parsing the JSON at runtime.

